Task 5 — bounded SearXNG runtime command supervision

Why:
- the old installer could deadlock on full stdout/stderr or outlive its work admission;
- one global deadline must cover all four runtime-build stages;
- diagnostics must stay private, bounded and secret-filtered.

Implementation:
- one command supervisor owns spawn, cancellation and the shared 300 s deadline;
- stdout/stderr drain concurrently into bounded 4 KiB tails;
- private atomic log is capped below 16 KiB and rejects links;
- parent-exit cleanup targets only inherited-pipe descendants: Unix signals the owned process group without resending the reaped root PID; Windows terminates only descendants still confined in the Job Object;
- visible failures remain generic while the private receipt records only a closed stage/category.

Controller review:
- found and fixed unbounded inherited-pipe waits, non-replacing Windows log writes and missing drain-error receipts;
- then found and fixed the Windows post-parent cleanup gap and the Unix PID-reuse exposure caused by releasing identity before signaling;
- split the post-parent authority to keep every production file at or below 230 lines.

Evidence:
- cargo test searxng::runtime_command --lib: 10/10;
- cargo test searxng::runtime --lib: 24/24;
- cargo test searxng::runtime_environment --lib: 12/12;
- cargo test process_tree --lib: 3/3;
- cargo fmt --check and strict Clippy green;
- x86_64-pc-windows-gnu cross-check green (pre-existing CEF dead-code warnings only);
- real Unix descendant fixture disappears before the global deadline;
- graphify update and git diff --check green.

Limits:
- Windows cleanup is cross-compiled here; its native behavioral execution belongs to the release-OS smoke in Task 7.
